{"qid":"Q185682","names":["French Indochina"],"title":"French Indochina","endonym":null,"capsule":"French Indochina was a federation of French protectorates and colonies in Southeast Asia — Cochinchina (a full colony from 1862), Annam, Tonkin, Cambodia, and Laos — unified under a Governor-General in Hanoi from 1887. The federation served as a major exporter of rice and rubber and was the scene of mounting nationalist resistance from the 1920s; Japanese occupation (1940–45) fatally undermined French authority, and the Viet Minh under Ho Chi Minh declared independence on 2 September 1945. The First Indochina War (1946–54) ended with the French defeat at Dien Bien Phu (May 1954) and the Geneva Accords, which partitioned Vietnam and recognized Cambodian and Laotian independence, effectively dissolving the federation.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. External boundaries of the federation are well defined by colonial treaties with Siam and China; internal administrative boundaries between protectorates are documented in French colonial records. The dataset's to-date of 1957 extends slightly beyond the 1954 Geneva settlement, likely reflecting residual French administrative presence.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Indochina","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Indochina"},{"label":"Wikipedia: French Indochina","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Indochina"}],"britannica":"topic/Indochinese-Union","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"French Indochina","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Ernest Constans","nativeName":"Ernest Constans","roman":"Ernest Constans","start":1887,"end":1888,"image":null,"